{"duration_api_ms":289202,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"563ac29c-05b9-4e70-a075-c0fdd9f925ee","total_cost_usd":0.14882900000000002,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10377,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":25613,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":22454},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10377,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":10,"output_tokens":25613,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10377,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10377},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":25613,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":10377,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.14882900000000002,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":22454,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\\n\\nAuthor: Priya Raghunathan\\nUpdated: 2026-03-18\\n\\n### Issue Description\\n\\nAppend requests to Nimbrel Ledger return HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`. Callers experience writes that will not land: Nimbrel Relay stalls, batch loaders resend the same envelope, and reads against the same shard begin to time out. The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\\n\\nA full refusal envelope from a batched append reads like this:\\n\\n```\\nappend batch refused after 0 of 64 envelopes: 429 Too Many Requests: {\\\"error\\\":{\\\"root_cause\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"nbl_queue_refused_exception\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"refused append on ingest path [ingest_and_leader_bytes=88121344, follower_bytes=212992, all_bytes=88334336, ingest_op_bytes=155648, max_ingest_bytes=88080384]\\\"}],\\\"type\\\":\\\"nbl_queue_refused_exception\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"refused append on ingest path [ingest_and_leader_bytes=88121344, follower_bytes=212992, all_bytes=88334336, ingest_op_bytes=155648, max_ingest_bytes=88080384]\\\"},\\\"status\\\":429}\\n```\\n\\n> **NOTE:** The Ledger operations handbook now covers the same ground under Refused Appends, with a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths set out below.\\n\\n### Environment\\n\\nEvery Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\\n\\n### Cause\\n\\nA [`429 - Too Many Requests` reply](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/http-status-codes) is what a Ledger node sends once it has nowhere left to put the work, which is to say once [an append queue or a read queue is full](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/why-appends-are-refused).\\n\\nThere are 3 ways an append draws a 429:\\n\\n- The `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for (`nbctl pool status --all`)\\n- The ingest memory guard has refused the batch (`nbctl guard report ingest --counters`). [1]\\n- A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`) - a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\\n\\nIn practice, expensive queries and deep grouping operations commonly contribute. A query that runs for several seconds holds a worker slot unavailable for other work, and the queue behind it grows. Grouping on high-cardinality fields yields a very large number of groups, and the memory that costs is charged to the same pool the append path draws on. When processor load looks unremarkable while memory sits high, this points toward query cost rather than an undersized cluster.\\n\\n[1] **From release 4.6 and release 5.1 onward**, appending to a `wide_text` column draws a 429 on its own account whenever the batch would otherwise have run the node out of memory.\\n\\n### Workaround\\n\\nTake append and query load off the cluster for long enough that the queues drain and the nodes fall back under their limits. Where batches carry `wide_text` columns and the cluster runs release 4.6 or release 5.1 or later, send fewer envelopes per batch.\\n\\nTo investigate refusals, set the slow query threshold to 1 second so that expensive queries are recorded individually instead of being averaged into statistics. Review the queries that run slowly with the team that wrote them; many can ask for less. While the refusal rate is high, capture a thread dump and a heap snapshot rather than reasoning from counters alone.\\n\\n### Resolution\\n\\nA cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry, so the answer is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out). Scale up first in most cases, and scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability, per [the sizing notes](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/sizing).\\n\\nSplitting a hot stream over more leader shards spreads append load across more nodes, which helps in some layouts.\\n\\nFor `wide_text` columns on release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later:\\n\\n1. Begin by cutting the number of envelopes in each append batch.\\n2. Where smaller batches do not clear it, add processor and memory capacity to the nodes.\\n3. Only then raise the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting, which defaults to 10% of the heap. A higher ceiling lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing, and a node holding too much runs out of memory instead of refusing, which is the worse outcome. The setting is read at startup, so the cluster must be restarted for a change to take.\\n\\nWhere none of that is open to you, the load itself is what changes: fewer appends, cheaper queries, or both. Refusals that show up only during a spike usually clear on their own once the queues drain.\\n\\n{internal-notes}\\n\\n- [Internal ticket NB-40917](https://tickets.nimbrel.example/issues/40917)\\n\\n{/internal-notes}\\n\\n- [Ledger slow query log](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/slow-query-log)\\n- [HTTP 429 replies](https://support.nimbrel.example/knowledge/318kqp2)\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\",\n          \"document\": \"source_a.md\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Rising 429 refusals on a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment\",\n          \"document\": \"source_b.md\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document's title covers the general phenomenon.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Append requests to Nimbrel Ledger return HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Changed 'come back' to 'return' for clarity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b1\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base title kept; B's title describes one case.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b2\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Author: Priya Raghunathan\\nUpdated: 2026-03-18\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base author maintained; B's case had different author.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b3\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Summary/TOC not part of base structure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Incident-specific context; general facts covered elsewhere.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"In practice, expensive queries and deep grouping operations commonly contribute.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Case-specific findings generalized into principles.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b6\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"### Environment\",\n      \"reason\": \"Duplicate section header; a10 kept.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b7\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Product info is instance of general fact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b8\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Version info is instance of general fact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b9\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Platform info is instance of general fact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b10\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Deployment type is instance of general fact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b11\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Production environment is instance of general fact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b12\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"### Issue Description\",\n      \"reason\": \"Duplicate section header; a3 kept.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b13\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Append requests to Nimbrel Ledger return HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Managed deployment case is instance of general phenomenon.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b14\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Real refusals confirmed in issue description.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b15\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Section header reorganized into Cause.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b16\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"A query that runs for several seconds holds a worker slot unavailable for other work, and the queue behind it grows.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Expensive query observation generalized to principle.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b17\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"A query that runs for several seconds holds a worker slot unavailable for other work, and the queue behind it grows.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Worker slot effect subsumed into cause explanation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b18\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Grouping on high-cardinality fields yields a very large number of groups, and the memory that costs is charged to the same pool the append path draws on.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Deep grouping example becomes general principle.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b19\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"When processor load looks unremarkable while memory sits high, this points toward query cost rather than an undersized cluster.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Resource accounting observation integrated into cause.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b20\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"When processor load looks unremarkable while memory sits high, this points toward query cost rather than an undersized cluster.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Diagnostic principle about query cost integrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b21\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Section header reorganized into Workaround.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b22\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"...set the slow query threshold to 1 second so that expensive queries are recorded individually instead of being averaged into statistics.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Recommendation becomes investigation step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b23\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Review the queries that run slowly with the team that wrote them; many can ask for less.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Recommendation becomes investigation step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b24\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"While the refusal rate is high, capture a thread dump and a heap snapshot rather than reasoning from counters alone.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Recommendation becomes investigation step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b25\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Section header not in base structure; links preserved.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\"\n}\n```","ttft_ms":255732,"type":"result","duration_ms":289260,"uuid":"566dbbfe-f62a-4036-9bf5-dc4071cf4183","ttft_stream_ms":857,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":858,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
